        reading: [
    {"id": "en_rd_a1_01", "level": "A1", "theme": "Daily life", "title": "A day at school", "text": "Maria is a student. She gets up at seven o’clock every morning. She has breakfast with her brother: bread, eggs and milk. Then she takes the bus to school. Her first class is at eight thirty. At noon, she eats lunch with her friends in the park. After school, she plays soccer for one hour. In the evening, she does her homework and goes to bed at ten.", "qs": [{"q": "What time does Maria get up?", "o": ["At six", "At seven", "At eight", "At eight thirty"], "a": 1, "why": "She gets up at seven o’clock every morning."}, {"q": "How does she go to school?", "o": ["By bus", "By car", "On foot", "By train"], "a": 0, "why": "She takes the bus to school."}, {"q": "What does she do after school?", "o": ["She watches TV", "She cooks dinner", "She plays soccer", "She goes shopping"], "a": 2, "why": "After school, she plays soccer for one hour."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_a2_01", "level": "A2", "theme": "Neighbours", "title": "A new neighbour", "text": "Last month, a new family moved into the apartment next to mine. The father, Daniel, works as a nurse at the city hospital, and the mother teaches music at an elementary school. They have two children, a girl of seven and a boy of four. On Sunday, they invited all the neighbours to a small lunch in the garden. Everyone brought something to share, so there was far too much food! I made a salad and a lemon cake. We talked for hours, and I think we are going to be good friends.", "qs": [{"q": "What does Daniel do for a living?", "o": ["He is a teacher", "He is a musician", "He is a cook", "He is a nurse"], "a": 3, "why": "Daniel works as a nurse at the city hospital."}, {"q": "Why was there too much food?", "o": ["They cooked for a week", "Everyone brought something", "The party was cancelled", "Only one family came"], "a": 1, "why": "Everyone brought something to share."}, {"q": "What did the narrator bring?", "o": ["A salad and a lemon cake", "Bread and cheese", "Only a cake", "Drinks"], "a": 0, "why": "The narrator made a salad and a lemon cake."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_b1_01", "level": "B1", "theme": "City", "title": "Bike sharing", "text": "Three years ago, the city of Vancouver launched a bike-sharing service available day and night. To use it, you sign up on an app and pay five dollars a month. The first thirty minutes of every trip are free; after that, the price is one dollar per additional half hour. The city hopes to reduce traffic downtown, where jams are frequent at rush hour. The project is not popular with everyone, however. Some shop owners fear that customers will come less often by car, while some residents complain about bikes left on sidewalks. To answer these concerns, the city has added two hundred stations and introduced a small fine for badly parked bikes.", "qs": [{"q": "How much is the monthly subscription?", "o": ["One dollar", "Thirty dollars", "Five dollars", "Nothing"], "a": 2, "why": "You pay five dollars a month."}, {"q": "What is the city’s main goal?", "o": ["To reduce downtown traffic", "To earn more money", "To build new roads", "To close shops"], "a": 0, "why": "The city hopes to reduce traffic downtown."}, {"q": "How has the city answered the complaints?", "o": ["It ended the service", "With more stations and a fine", "It raised the prices", "It banned cars"], "a": 1, "why": "It added two hundred stations and a fine for badly parked bikes."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_b2_01", "level": "B2", "theme": "Work", "title": "Remote work and productivity", "text": "Remote work has become permanent for many companies, yet its effects remain hotly debated. A recent survey of two thousand employees found that productivity rose slightly among people who work from home two or three days a week. However, workers who stayed at home full time reported more isolation and fewer chances of promotion. Managers also admit that spontaneous exchanges, which often generate new ideas, are harder to reproduce on a screen. For this reason, a growing number of firms now favour a hybrid model, in which teams meet in person on fixed days. Experts caution that this compromise only works if the rules are clear and applied fairly to everyone.", "qs": [{"q": "What did the survey find about people working from home two or three days a week?", "o": ["Productivity dropped sharply", "Productivity rose slightly", "Nothing changed", "They quit more often"], "a": 1, "why": "Productivity rose slightly for part-time remote workers."}, {"q": "What did full-time remote workers report?", "o": ["Higher salaries", "Better teamwork", "More isolation and fewer promotions", "Shorter commutes only"], "a": 2, "why": "They reported more isolation and fewer chances of promotion."}, {"q": "When does the hybrid model work, according to experts?", "o": ["When the rules are clear and fair", "When everyone stays home", "When meetings are optional", "When offices are bigger"], "a": 0, "why": "The compromise only works if the rules are clear and applied fairly."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_c1_01", "level": "C1", "theme": "Society", "title": "Artificial intelligence and employment", "text": "The rise of artificial intelligence is deeply reshaping the labour market. While some repetitive tasks are bound to disappear, economists point out that earlier technological revolutions also created jobs that nobody had imagined. The real challenge, they argue, lies in the speed of the transition: retraining workers takes years, whereas software can be deployed in a matter of weeks. Moreover, the benefits are unevenly distributed, since highly qualified professionals tend to gain from these tools, whereas less qualified workers risk being left behind. Hence the call for public investment in lifelong training, rather than a simple ban on the technology.", "qs": [{"q": "What do economists recall about earlier technological revolutions?", "o": ["They destroyed every job", "They had no effect", "Workers rejected them", "They also created unforeseen jobs"], "a": 3, "why": "Earlier revolutions created jobs nobody had imagined."}, {"q": "According to the text, what is the real challenge?", "o": ["The speed of the transition", "The cost of software", "The lack of engineers", "The shortage of computers"], "a": 0, "why": "Retraining takes years, whereas software arrives in weeks."}, {"q": "What solution does the text suggest?", "o": ["Banning the technology", "Public investment in lifelong training", "Lowering wages", "Stopping all retraining"], "a": 1, "why": "It calls for public investment in lifelong training."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_a1_02", "level": "A1", "theme": "Food", "title": "Dinner with friends", "text": "Today is Friday. Ben has dinner with two friends at his apartment. He cooks pasta with tomato sauce and a big green salad. His friend Lucy brings a chocolate cake. The other friend, Omar, brings juice and fruit. They eat, talk and laugh. At ten o’clock, the friends go home, and Ben washes the dishes.", "qs": [{"q": "What does Ben cook?", "o": ["Soup and bread", "Pasta and a salad", "Rice and chicken", "Pizza"], "a": 1, "why": "He cooks pasta with tomato sauce and a green salad."}, {"q": "Who brings the cake?", "o": ["Omar", "Ben", "Lucy", "Nobody"], "a": 2, "why": "Lucy brings a chocolate cake."}, {"q": "What does Ben do at the end?", "o": ["He goes to bed", "He washes the dishes", "He calls Omar", "He goes out"], "a": 1, "why": "The friends go home, and Ben washes the dishes."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_a2_02", "level": "A2", "theme": "Travel", "title": "Train delays", "text": "Marta takes the train to work every day. Yesterday, the train was forty minutes late because of a problem with the signals. At the station, an announcement said that the next train would leave at eight fifteen. Many passengers were angry, but the staff gave out free coffee and apologized. Marta called her manager to explain. When she finally arrived at the office, the meeting had already started, so she sat quietly at the back.", "qs": [{"q": "Why was the train late?", "o": ["Because of the weather", "Because of a signal problem", "Because of a strike", "Because it was full"], "a": 1, "why": "There was a problem with the signals."}, {"q": "What did the staff do?", "o": ["They gave out coffee", "They gave refunds", "They cancelled the train", "They closed the station"], "a": 0, "why": "The staff gave out free coffee and apologized."}, {"q": "Where did Marta sit at the meeting?", "o": ["At the front", "Next to her manager", "At the back", "She did not go"], "a": 2, "why": "She sat quietly at the back."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_a2_03", "level": "A2", "theme": "Shopping", "title": "Returning a jacket", "text": "Last week, Tom bought a blue jacket in a shop downtown. At home, he noticed that it was too small, so he went back to the shop with the receipt. The seller said he could exchange it for a bigger size or take a store credit, but he could not get his money back. Tom chose the bigger size. It was a little more expensive, so he paid the difference. Now he wears the jacket every day.", "qs": [{"q": "Why did Tom return the jacket?", "o": ["The colour was ugly", "It was too small", "It was broken", "It was too expensive"], "a": 1, "why": "He noticed that it was too small."}, {"q": "What could Tom not do?", "o": ["Exchange it", "Get a store credit", "Get his money back", "Return it with the receipt"], "a": 2, "why": "He could not get his money back."}, {"q": "What did Tom pay at the end?", "o": ["Nothing", "The full price again", "The price difference", "Half the price"], "a": 2, "why": "The bigger size cost a little more, so he paid the difference."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_b1_02", "level": "B1", "theme": "Work", "title": "A new schedule", "text": "Last spring, a small company in Calgary tried a four-day work week. Employees kept the same salary but worked ten hours a day from Monday to Thursday. After six months, the manager reported that productivity had not fallen and that fewer people were calling in sick. However, some workers found the long days exhausting, especially those with young children. For this reason, the company is now testing a more flexible model, in which each person can choose between two different schedules.", "qs": [{"q": "What did the employees keep?", "o": ["The same salary", "The same hours per day", "Their Fridays", "Their old schedule"], "a": 0, "why": "Employees kept the same salary."}, {"q": "What did the manager notice after six months?", "o": ["Lower productivity", "Fewer sick days", "More complaints about salary", "Less work"], "a": 1, "why": "Productivity had not fallen and fewer people were calling in sick."}, {"q": "Why is the company testing a new model?", "o": ["Because of the cost", "Because the long days were hard for some", "Because sales fell", "Because the manager left"], "a": 1, "why": "Some workers found the long days exhausting."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_b1_03", "level": "B1", "theme": "Environment", "title": "A community garden", "text": "In a neighbourhood of Halifax, residents have turned an empty lot into a community garden. Anyone can rent a small plot for twenty dollars a year and grow vegetables or flowers. The project began when an elderly woman asked the city for permission to plant tomatoes behind her building. Soon, her neighbours joined her. Today, the garden has forty plots, a waiting list and a small market every Sunday. Beyond the food, the organizers say the main benefit is that people who never spoke to each other now meet every week.", "qs": [{"q": "How much does a plot cost?", "o": ["Nothing", "Twenty dollars a year", "Forty dollars", "Twenty dollars a month"], "a": 1, "why": "A plot costs twenty dollars a year."}, {"q": "How did the project start?", "o": ["A company donated land", "The city built it", "A woman asked to plant tomatoes", "A school proposed it"], "a": 2, "why": "An elderly woman asked the city for permission to plant tomatoes."}, {"q": "What do the organizers see as the main benefit?", "o": ["Cheaper vegetables", "More tourists", "A way for neighbours to meet", "A new market"], "a": 2, "why": "People who never spoke to each other now meet every week."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_b2_02", "level": "B2", "theme": "Technology", "title": "Smartphones in classrooms", "text": "Schools around the world are divided over smartphones. Supporters argue that the devices give students instant access to information and prepare them for a digital world. Opponents, however, point to studies showing that the mere presence of a phone on a desk reduces concentration, even when it is switched off. Several education ministries have therefore banned phones during lessons, while others have chosen to teach responsible use instead. Early results from schools that adopted a ban are mixed: grades have improved slightly, but teachers report that enforcing the rule takes considerable effort.", "qs": [{"q": "What do opponents claim?", "o": ["Phones are too expensive", "A phone on the desk lowers concentration", "Students do not like phones", "Phones damage hearing"], "a": 1, "why": "The mere presence of a phone on a desk reduces concentration."}, {"q": "What did some ministries do?", "o": ["Gave phones to students", "Banned phones in class", "Closed schools", "Taxed phones"], "a": 1, "why": "Several ministries banned phones during lessons."}, {"q": "What do the early results show?", "o": ["A clear success", "A total failure", "Mixed results", "No results yet"], "a": 2, "why": "The text calls them mixed: slightly better grades, but hard enforcement."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_b2_03", "level": "B2", "theme": "Health", "title": "Sleep and memory", "text": "Although many people regard sleep as lost time, scientists increasingly see it as an active period during which the brain organizes what it has learned during the day. In one experiment, students who slept after studying a list of words remembered considerably more of them the next morning than those who stayed awake. Researchers concluded that sleep helps transfer information from short-term to long-term memory. Nevertheless, they warn that these findings do not mean that longer sleep is always better: what matters most is regularity, since an irregular schedule seems to weaken the benefits.", "qs": [{"q": "How do scientists increasingly see sleep?", "o": ["As lost time", "As an active period for the brain", "As a rest for the body only", "As a bad habit"], "a": 1, "why": "They see it as an active period in which the brain organizes what it has learned."}, {"q": "What did the students who slept do better?", "o": ["Studied more words", "Remembered more words", "Finished sooner", "Felt happier"], "a": 1, "why": "They remembered considerably more words the next morning."}, {"q": "What matters most according to the researchers?", "o": ["Sleeping longer", "Sleeping during the day", "A regular schedule", "Studying at night"], "a": 2, "why": "What matters most is regularity."}]},
    {"id": "en_rd_c1_02", "level": "C1", "theme": "Culture", "title": "Translation and meaning", "text": "Translators often say that their work is less a matter of replacing words than of recreating an effect. A joke, a rhythm or a cultural reference rarely survives a literal rendering; what is needed instead is an equivalent that produces the same response in a reader who does not share the original context. This is why the same novel can feel remarkably different in two translations, each defensible in its own right. Far from being a flaw, this plurality may be the best argument for reading translated works with a measure of curiosity rather than suspicion.", "qs": [{"q": "According to translators, what is their work mainly about?", "o": ["Replacing each word", "Recreating an effect", "Correcting the author", "Shortening the text"], "a": 1, "why": "Their work is less about replacing words than about recreating an effect."}, {"q": "Why can a novel feel different in two translations?", "o": ["One of them contains errors", "Each can be defensible in its own way", "The readers are different ages", "The titles change"], "a": 1, "why": "Each translation can be defensible in its own right."}, {"q": "What does the author suggest about this plurality?", "o": ["It is a flaw to be fixed", "It should be avoided", "It is a reason to read with curiosity", "It is very rare"], "a": 2, "why": "It may be the best argument for reading translations with curiosity rather than suspicion."}]}
        ]
